course: dictionary
lesson: 10812
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show you which one the writer means. This is context again. «The factory filled a vat with warm milk for the cheese.» Factory, milk and cheese tell you a vat is a big tank.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. «The price includes VAT, so you pay the tax as well.» Price, tax and shop tell you VAT means money. The capital letters are a clue too: VAT is short for value added tax.
explain: Test your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «In the UK, VAT is already included in the price.» If you put a tank there, it makes no sense. If you put a tax there, it makes sense, so the tax meaning is right.
fact: A vat is a very large tank or barrel for holding liquids.
fact: Vats are used in factories, breweries and dye works, to hold things like wine, beer or dye.
fact: «A vat of» is a common phrase, as in «a vat of dye».
fact: VAT stands for value added tax, a tax added to the price of most things you buy in shops.
fact: In the UK, VAT is often already included in the price on the label, and you say the letters V-A-T, or say «vat».
exercise: «The workers stirred the dye in the huge vat.» What is a vat? | A small pot for cooking soup | A type of hat | A sweet, sticky drink | *A very large tank for holding liquids
exercise: «Some tax hides in shop prices, like VAT.» What is VAT? | A rule about school holidays | A large barrel for storing water | *A tax added to the price of most things you buy in shops | A type of cheese
exercise: In «a huge vat full of microbes», what does vat most likely mean? | A kind of grass | A kind of bird | A tiny living thing | *A large tank that holds things
test: «In the UK, VAT is already included in the price.» What does VAT mean here? | A sort of tiny bird | A type of microbe | *A tax added to the price of things you buy | A large tank for milk
test: Priya says vat means "a big tank" in «plus VAT». Is she right? | No, because VAT is always a tank | Yes, because plus means a tank | *No, here VAT is a tax, not a tank | Yes, because a tank can be a tax
exam: Which sentence uses vat to mean a tank, not a tax? | *«The brewer filled the vat with beer.» | «VAT is at twenty per cent.» | «The price includes VAT.» | «Add VAT to the total.»
